Captain hold terminal warning decision - 2026-10-06

Binding captain decision
Hold suppresses automatic wake turns only.
Do not hide the terminal supervision-failure warning during hold.
Restore the existing once-only terminal warning ownership and stdout behavior; remove the R2-1 suppression/rollback machinery instead of adding coordination.
The runtime contract is owned by docs/turnend-guard.md; docs/supervision-protocols/claude.md points there.

Current ledger
R1-1: held; canonical routed operational classification retained.
R1-2: accepted by captain on 2026-10-06; narrow final EXIT cleanup-window race remains accepted, no coordination authorized, do not re-raise.
R2-1: dissolved under the revised warning contract; warning suppression and compensating rollback removed.
R3-1: dissolved by removal of that rollback; no ownership-token patch or new coordination.
The round-2, R2-1 fix and round-3 summaries are historical evidence and explicitly point to this decision.

Cause and structural correction
Applying hold to diagnostic stdout created a consumed-but-hidden warning; invocation-local rollback of the shared alarm pathname then allowed an old handler to remove a newer episode's alarm.
The fix removes both the suppression and rollback, so no handler cleans up an alarm pathname after releasing ownership.
Held entry reads the existing budget without incrementing it and uses the same verified terminal check as unheld entry.
The original notice, failed epoch, matching session, exhausted budget, owner-before-budget locks, live-owner exclusion, final watcher recheck and exclusive alarm claim remain intact.
No state, ownership tokens or coordination are added.
Removed state: terminal EXIT rollback and successful-output disarm.
Local main and pre-rollback 77190d9 retain an alarm claim if stdout fails and return 0 after the attempted output; the revised implementation restores that exact behavior.

Red before production edit
Starting production HEAD: 206ac23; branch fm/fm-captain-hold-marker; local-main merge-base 25dee3c5f94ed13f6e563a54f58da547135d6d84.
Held-at-entry test failed: held-at-entry terminal warning was hidden.
Late-hold test failed: late hold hid terminal warning.
Closed-stdout test failed: stdout failure rolled back the original once-only claim.
Both replacement-episode tests failed: old terminal cleanup deleted the replacement alarm.
All five failures were intended behavioral assertions, not setup failures.

Green and regression evidence
Held-at-entry warning delivered once; subsequent held Stops silent 0; budget, marker and queue unchanged; no arm or exit-2 continuation; dismissal does not repeat the warning.
Eligibility matrix passes: exact budget boundary, missing or malformed budget, mismatched session, missing notice/epoch, rewake epoch, away ownership, live auto-arm owner, budget contention, no supervision need and healthy watcher.
Late hold delivers its warning once; positive recovery clears the failure episode.
Closed stdout retains the original claim and allow semantics.
Both original reviewer replacement-alarm reproductions retain the newer alarm and report duplicate_warning=no; the held outer output is delivered, and closed stdout is reported without rollback.
